823:
(a) General.
(1) The procedure for review and approval of a subdivision plat consists of 2 separate steps. The initial step is the preparation and submission to the Planning Commission of a preliminary plat of the proposed subdivision or development. The second step is the preparation and submission to the Planning Commission of a final plat, together with required certificates. All Multifamily Subdivisions or developments shall be required to submit full plans, including location of buildings, layout of buildings, footprints of the buildings, etc., to the Planning Commission for approval.
(2) The final plat becomes the instrument to be recorded in the office of the Clerk of the Superior Court of Walker County, when duly signed by the Chairperson of the Planning Commission. All final plats approved by the Planning Commission must be stamped and in recordable form under the laws of the State of Georgia.
(b) Pre-application. The subdivider/developer must consult early and informally with the Planning Director and staff to obtain advice and assistance before he begins to prepare the preliminary plat. This will enable him to become familiar with the County Thoroughfare Plan and other official plans, which might affect the area.
(c) Preliminary plat approval. Prior to making any street improvements or installing any utilities, the subdivider/developer shall submit the complete subdivision or development plan for preliminary approval, although he may plan to construct only a portion of it initially. These plans must be complete in that they include building layouts and locations, floor plans, street plans, etc.
(1) The application for preliminary plat approval, including 6 prints of complete subdivision plans, shall be submitted to the secretary of the Planning Commission at least 20 days prior to the Planning Commission's regularly scheduled meeting on the second Thursday of each month.
(2) The Planning Commission, before its review, may transmit prints of the preliminary plat to each utility service, Health Department, Fire Department, and any other agency for review and recommendations in relation to specific service problems. A print of the preliminary plat may also be transmitted to the Coosa Valley Regional Development Center for its review and recommendations.
(3) Prior to a decision on the preliminary plat, the Planning Commission shall afford a public hearing. Notice of the time and place of such hearing shall be sent by mail to names as they appear on the most current tax records obtained from the Walker County Assessor's Office. Notice of the public hearing shall be sent at least 5 days prior to the date of the hearing.
(4) The preliminary plat, which shall meet the minimum standard of design and the general requirements for the construction of public improvements set forth in these regulations, shall give the following information:
a. Vicinity sketch map at a scale of 1 inch equals 800 feet showing the following:
1. Name and location of subdivision or development;
2. North point, graphic scale, and date;
3. Boundaries and approximate dimensions;
4. Amount of acreage to be subdivided;
5. Major traffic arteries, utilities, and community facilities (major shopping areas, schools, parks, hospitals, churches) which are pertinent to the proposed design.
b. Preliminary plat of subdivision at a scale of not more than 1 inch equals 100 feet (unless otherwise approved by the Planning Director) showing the following:
1. Name and location of subdivision;
2. Names and addresses of owners and persons to be notified of official action;
3. North point, graphic scale, and date (north point shall be identified as magnetic, true, or grid north);
4. Topography at a 5-foot contour interval (unless a closer interval is required by the Planning Commission);
5. Street plan which includes:
(i) Location of all existing and proposed streets within the subdivision and adjacent to it;
(ii) Widths and purpose of existing and proposed rights-of-way and easements, any proposed rights-of-ways, easements, or private drives shall be brought to County specifications;
(iii) Clear identification of right-of-way location and width for any street which is considered as part of the Sketch Thoroughfare Plan;
(iv) Street names which are subject to approval by the Planning Commission and verified as not duplicated by the E 9-1-1 staff;
(v) Typical cross section of proposed streets showing natural and finished grades;
(vi) Off-street parking. Off-street parking shall be provided on the same lot or on a lot adjacent to the structure it serves at a rate of 2 spaces per dwelling unit. Units of 4 bedrooms or more shall be required to have 3 parking spaces;
6. Blocks and lots with dimensions shown for all lot lines or property boundaries;
7. Building setback lines along each street;
8. Proposed building locations;
9. Proposed floor plans;
10. Feasible connections to existing or proposed utility systems and any proposed improvements requested of the County. When such connections are not practical, all proposed individual water supply and/or sewage disposal systems shall meet the approval of the Health Department;
11. Plans of all proposed utility layouts which included the following:
(i) Preliminary plan of sanitary sewers (if applicable) with grade, pipe size, and points of discharge or soil tests as specified by the Health Department (prepared by the owner's engineer with seal affixed);
(ii) Preliminary plan of water supply system (if applicable) with pipe sizes and location of hydrants (prepared by a professional engineer with seal affixed);
(iii) Preliminary plan of storm sewer system with grade, pipe sizes and location of outlet subject to review and approval by the Walker County Road Superintendent (prepared by the owner's engineer with seal affixed);
12. Location and dimension of land to be dedicated or reserved for parks, open space, or other public use;
13. Any portion of the land in the subdivision or development subject to periodic inundation by storm drainage, overflow, or ponding shall be clearly shown and identified on the plat, including, but not limited to, what shows on the 100-year flood maps;
14. The existing zoning classification of subdivision and all contiguous land. If the proposed subdivision does not lie within the force and effect of the existing zoning ordinance, the preliminary plat shall be accompanied by a plan indicating the proposed use of the lots and a copy of the instrument to be used in establishing restrictions;
15. The names of adjacent subdivisions and the names and address of record owners of adjoining parcels of land as they appear on the current tax records;
16. In case of re-subdivision, a copy of the existing plat with the proposed re-subdivision superimposed thereon;
17. The original layout of the parent map and parcel with any division from that parcel shown, along with the map and parcel numbers;
18. Zoning of subject property and adjacent properties;
19. A minimum of 10 percent useable land shall be dedicated to open space/recreational areas, not to include the individual yards. For every 50 units, and additional 5 percent useable land shall be dedicated to open space/recreational area. Incentives will be given for any voluntary additional dedicated open space/recreational areas within the development at the discretion of the Planning Commission. Detention ponds, wetlands, steep slopes, or any other areas required by law to have or to protect shall not be allowable uses or shall not be counted toward open space/recreational areas;
20. A Soil Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan completed in accordance with the Walker County Soil Erosion and Sedimentation Control Ordinance (including hydrology);
21. A Stormwater Pollution Prevention Plan shall be completed in accordance with the post-development stormwater management for new or redevelopment ordinance; and
22. As part of any development plan, the following documents must be filed for approval with the Walker County Planning Commission as a part of the preliminary development plan:
(i) The form of all agreements between the developer and property owners relative to development standards and property ownership and common area ownership and maintenance with written statement by the County Attorney that this document has been reviewed by that office. If there is to be a single owner of the development, a maintenance schedule of the grounds, commons areas, etc., must be submitted for approval;
(ii) The form of all agreements between and among individual property owners relative to the ownership and maintenance of privately owned properties and common areas with written statement by the County Attorney that this document has been reviewed by that office. If there is to be a single owner of the development, a maintenance schedule of the grounds, commons areas, etc. must be submitted for approval;
(iii) Certification by the Walker County Planning Director that all improvements have been installed in accordance with the submitted plan or certification by the developer of the existence of surety in favor of the individual property owner sufficient to assure the completion of the submitted plan.
(5) Within 30 days after the first regularly scheduled meeting of the Planning Commission, which is held 15 or more days after the submission of the preliminary plat, the Planning Commission shall review the plat and indicate its approval, disapproval, or approval subject to any required modifications unless the applicant for the Planning Commission's approval waives this requirement and consents to the extension of such period. If the plat is disapproved or is approved subject to modifications, the reasons for such disapproval or the required modifications shall be indicated in writing on 2 copies of the plat and shall be entered upon the minutes of the Planning Commission.
(6) One copy of the preliminary plat shall be retained in the Planning Commission files, and 1 copy shall be returned to the subdivider at the time of approval or disapproval, with the Planning Commission's stamp, signed by the Chairperson of the Planning Commission or another duly qualified member of the Commission, along with any required changes or modifications endorsed thereon. Failure of the Planning Commission to consider any preliminary plat within these 30 days, or such period as extended, shall be considered as approval of same as submitted.
(7) Approval of the preliminary plat by the Planning Commission shall not constitute acceptance of the final plat. Approval of the preliminary plat shall lapse unless a final plat, of all or part of the area shown on the preliminary plat, in substantial conformance therewith, is submitted within 12 months from the date of such approval, unless an extension of time is specifically applied for by the subdivider/developer and expressly granted by the Planning Commission.
(d) Construction of improvements. After the preliminary plat has been approved, the subdivider shall elect 1 of the following courses of action:
(1) Install all required improvements. Installation shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat as approved; and if desired by the subdivider, it may constitute only that portion of the approved preliminary plat which he proposes to record for final plat; provided, however, that such portion conforms to all the requirements of these regulations, including, but not limited to, the Soil Erosion and Sedimentation Control Ordinance, the post-development stormwater management for new or redevelopment, and the Street Design Ordinance;
(2) Deposit cash or post a surety bond with the Walker County Board of Commissioners' Office in an amount sufficient to assure the completion of all required improvements as well as all other surety requirements as listed in section 22-407 (surety for completion of improvements).
After the completion of either 1 of the previous alternatives, approval and recording of the final plat, the subdivider may proceed with the sale of lots.
(e) Final plat approval. Final plat approval shall be obtained by the subdivider as the preliminary plat or portions of it are constructed or included in the surety bond. The final plat shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat as approved; and if submitted in portions, each portion shall conform to all requirements of these standards. If the subdivider chooses to do the final plat in phases, any changes made to the plat must also consider post construction stormwater control measures.
(1) At least 20 days prior to the meeting at which it is to be considered, the subdivider shall submit 6 copies (black and white or blue line prints), together with any street profiles or other plans and any certifications which may be required by the Planning Commission. Any copies that the subdivider wishes to retain for his records should be submitted in addition to the required minimum.
(2) Prior to a decision on the final plat, the Planning Commission shall afford a public hearing. Notice of the public hearing shall be sent at least 5 days prior to the date of the hearing.
(3) The plat shall be drawn to a scale of 1 inch equals 100 feet (unless otherwise approved by the Planning Director), on sheets not larger than 17 by 20 inches. When more than 1 sheet is required, an index sheet of the same size shall be filed as a key, showing the entire subdivision, with the sheets in alphabetical order.
(4) The final plat shall give the following information:
a. Vicinity sketch map at a scale of 1 inch equals 800 feet showing the site in relation to its surroundings. The map submitted with the preliminary plat may be used.
b. Final plat of the subdivision including the following:
1. Name and location of subdivision;
2. Name and address of owner and person to be notified of official action;
3. North point, graphic scale and date; north point shall be identified as magnetic, true or grid north;
4. Location sketch map showing relationship of the subdivision to the surrounding area;
5. Plan and profile of all streets and names of all streets, roads and alleys and other rights-of-way;
6. Total number of lots, total acreage, total length of new streets;
7. Location of all blocks and lot lines with all block and lot numbers in numerical order;
8. Building setback lines along each street;
9. Sufficient data to determine readily and reproduce on the ground the location, bearing, and length of every road line, boundary line, block line, and building line, whether curved or straight, and including the radius, central angle, and tangent distance, and the length of curve for the center line of all curved roads and property lines which are not the boundary of the property lines being subdivided. The length of all dimensions shall be to the nearest 100th of 1 foot, and bearings of all angles to the nearest 1 minute;
10. Location, dimensions, and purpose of all easements;
11. Location and description of all monuments and iron pins;
12. Final plat of storm sewer system with grade, pipe sizes, and location of outlets (prepared by a certified professional engineer or certified surveyor with seal affixed);
13. Final plan of water supply system (if applicable) with pipe sizes and location of hydrants and valves;
14. Computations showing closure of tract boundary which closure shall be 1 foot in 5,000 feet as a minimum;
15. The parent map and parcel with any division from that parcel shown, along with the parent map and parcel numbers.
(5) The following certifications shall be presented along with the final plat:
a. Certification by the subdivider showing that the applicant is the legal owner of the land, and that he formally dedicates all streets, rights-of-way, and any other sites for public use.
b. Certification by a registered land surveyor (seal affixed) of the accuracy of the survey and plat, and the placement of all required monuments.
c. Certification of approval by the Health Department of the water supply and sewage systems installed or proposed to be installed, stating that they fully meet the requirements of the Health Department regulations.
d. Certification by the appropriate County Authority that the subdivider has complied with 1 of the following alternatives:
1. Installed all improvements according to the requirements of these regulations; or
2. Deposited cash or posted a surety bond with the Walker County Board of Commissioners in an amount sufficient to assure completion of all required improvements.
e. Certificate of approval by the Georgia Department of Transportation for projects abutting any Federal or State roadways and/or Certificate of Approval by the Walker County Road Department for any projects abutting any County roadways.
f. Certification of Inspection by the Project Engineer who designed the approved Stormwater Pollution Prevention Plan that the site has been inspected and is installed according to the approved plan or that modifications to the plan are designed and installed using sound engineering principles and practices with no adverse effects on any adjacent property.
g. Certification by the Walker County Environmental Manager, or representative thereof, that the approved Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan has been completed and adhered to and final stabilization has been reached.
h. Certification of approval to be signed by the Secretary of the Planning Commission.
i. A Certification from the County Board of Commissioners indicating that sufficient bond has been posted.
(6) When the plat has been approved by the Planning Commission, 1 copy with the approval of the Planning Commission certified thereon shall be submitted for filing with the Clerk of the Superior Court within 10 days as the official plat of record. Copies of the approved final plat will be delivered by the Planning Commission to a minimum of the following locations, if applicable:
a. 2 copies of plat to the Zoning and Development Department.
b. 1 copy of plat to the Walker County Assessors' Office.
c. 1 copy of plat to Emergency 911 Office.
d. 1 copy of plat to Health Department (if property shall be serviced by septic systems).
Any copies that the subdivider wishes to retain for his records should be submitted in addition to the required minimum. The Planning Commission must consider a final plat within 30 days after its first regularly scheduled meeting, which is held 15 or more days after the submission of the final plat. Failure of the Planning Commission to act on a final plat submission within these 30 days shall be considered as approval of same. If the plat is denied, it shall be in writing in the official minutes of the Planning Commission and shall be stated in writing on 2 copies of the plat. One copy of the plat shall be retained in the Planning Commission files and 1 copy shall be returned to the subdivider within 5 days of the disapproval.
(7) Approval of a final plat by the Planning Commission shall not constitute acceptance by the County Board of Commissioners of the dedication of any street or other public way or ground. After approval of the final plat and the construction of streets shown thereon, the Planning Commission may recommend to the Governing Body that it accept these streets as public roads and take over their perpetual maintenance at the request of the subdivider following 1 year from the date of the final plat approval.
(Ord. of 6-24-2004(3), § 9.36)

